Nat Nina 

### Concept & Implementation
A journaling machine where the user writes out a journal entry. While they write, the system quietly tracks how they write: where they hesitate, which words they delete or rewrite, and so on. When the entry is finished, it reveals everything it picked up and offers an interpretation of the writer's character based on those behaviours. I found it interesiting as a concept and it's pontential uses for future purposes, and I also thought the design was pretty suiting. The presentation was well done and showed a lot of the project's inner-workings and core idea. Some suggestions: Let users replay their writing session as an animation, so they can watch their own pauses and deletions. Compare entries over time to show how someone's writing habits change with mood or date. 



### Concept & Implementation
An interactive app where passing clouds drift across the screen and users type in what shape they think each cloud resembles. The most popular answers are then displayed around the clouds, so the shared interpretations become part of the visual. [Add anything you remember about how answers were stored or ranked. Cloud-gazing is something nearly everyone has done, so the interaction needed no explanation which is nice. Showing the most popular answers turns individual imagination into a shared experience, and it makes the viewer curious about what others saw. 
Some suggestions: Let users draw or outline the shape they see, not just type it. Keep an archive of past clouds and their answers so the piece grows over time.



